es a migration, a second RLS policy, a foreign key and its cascade, an entire cross-table deadlock analysis, and deletion propagation as a separate concern — and it makes invalidation free, because the rebuild nulls the columns in the same statement that rewrites content.\n- **D5** — a dedicated embed queue, enqueued from **every** projection-changing path including the inline Tier-1 finalize rebuild. That rebuild runs no queue job, so enqueueing only from the reindex worker would mean an ordinary turn never embeds until a sweep noticed it.\n- **D6** — no completeness gate. Retrieval fuses whatever legs exist, with a per-row `embedding_model_key` filter so vectors from different models never meet in one ranking. The gate in the earlier draft guarded a flip between two resident models; with one column there is no flip to guard.\n- **D10** — embedding lag needs its own BYPASSRLS discovery predicate. `llame_search_stale_chats` compares `chats.updated_at` to `indexed_at`, so a fully-indexed never-embedded corpus returns **zero rows** — the same silent failure that left 7 of 8 chats unindexed in phase 1.\n- **D14** — a fourth worker group, `search-embed`, so embedding concurrency is an operator dial rather than a source constant. It is network-bound and latency-tolerant; `search-reindex` is DB-bound and latency-sensitive. One concurrency number cannot serve both.\n- **D16** — terminal failures tombstoned in place, with the three states derived from the columns rather than stored. Without it a permanently unembeddable document loops forever: retries exhaust, the job dead-letters, discovery still reports the chat as lagging, and the sweep re-enqueues — unbounded spend producing no error anyone reads.\n\n## Three traps recorded with tests that assert them\n\nThese pass a naive suite and are the likeliest ways this ships broken. They are called out in `tasks.md` so they are not mistaken for redundant coverage:\n\n1. The rebuild upserts on `(chat_id, chunk_ordinal, chunker_version)`. An `ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE` omitting the embedding columns **preserves a stale vector across a content change** — the one silent-wrong-answer path in the design.\n2. The coverage predicate must use `IS DISTINCT FROM`, not `=`. With `=`, a never-embedded row compares NULL against the model key, the negated conjunction yields NULL, and the row is silently excluded — never-embedded documents would never be discovered.\n3. The embed worker must write only embedding columns. Touching `chats.updated_at` or `search_chat_state` would let the lexical predicate re-flag the chat, creating a rebuild/embed feedback loop.\n\n## Dependencies discovered while designing\n\n- **#517** — `chunkByCharBudget` always takes at least one item, so a message larger than `CHUNK_MAX_CHARS` is emitted as one oversized document. Harmless lexically, unembeddable in practice. Fixing it now costs one projection rebuild; fixing it after embeddings ship costs a rebuild **and** a full re-embed.\n- **#518** — model-generated chunk context, deliberately deferred behind #517's free deterministic anchor so the lift is measured rather than assumed.\n\n## Known limits, stated rather than glossed\n\n- **BREAKING (deployment):** Postgres must provide the `vector` extension. Self-hosters change their image before upgrading.\n- A model swap re-embeds in place rather than backfilling in parallel — semantic recall is weaker until it completes, degrading toward lexical, which is the shipped state anyway.\n- Two open questions remain, both deferrable: whether role labels help or hurt the vector (settled by an A/B in #197, reversible for one `embed_input_version` bump), and which model to document as the default (decided against the extended eval dataset in the first layer).\n- Two implementation lookups gate real decisions: pgvector's storage class for the `vector` type, and which pg-boss v12 queue fields are immutable after `createQueue`.\n\nRefs #194, #196, #517, #518\nEOF\ngh pr edit 522 --title \"docs(s